{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1462201-exalted-3e-map-speculation-help","id":1462201,"title":"Exalted 3E Map Speculation/Help","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-09-30T19:07:47-07:00","id":1462201,"title":"Exalted 3E Map Speculation/Help","content":"Hello all!<br /> <br /> I posted this to Reddit, but I thought I'd ask here too. Most people probably don't know me, <a href=\"https://imgur.com/gallery/OWk3CED\" target=\"_blank\">but they might know my map</a>. Any who, as someone who is not a fan of Exalted 3E's system, but really likes what they did with the map (obviously) and the new lore, maybe Reddit can help me puzzle out some things and get some help from those more in the loop. Using Lea Sheppard's 1E/2E map as a guide, it's obvious some of the places in 3E just do not line up with the old map. Two of the most egregious examples of this are Harborhead and An-Teng. Old Harborhead is covered by a new system of huge lakes (or inland seas) and An-Teng's geography does not like up with its old coastline at all.<br /> <br /> Now, this is not unusual. Most places don't really line up anymore from one map to the other. Whitewall got moved about 500 miles to the East, Chiaroscuro got a little nudge (but not huge), and hell, the Isle of Eternal Leaves and Tongma Island simply ceased to be. But what stands out to me is that some of these places were very, VERY fleshed out in 1E and 2E, especially in 1E for Harborhead in Houses of the Bull God.<br /> <br /> But now (especially for Harborhead) the geography seems to stand in the way of the old lore, particularly when describing Harborhead's back country, mountains and perhaps critically, the Bent Creek jade beds. So, thoughts on reconciling the two? Or, am I making an Imperial Mountain out of a molehill.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Map of Third Edition Creation in a First Edition Style<